Camera media child ownership on Linux
=====================================

HLS FFmpeg and both FFprobe discovery paths launch through a small stdlib-only
helper. It registers Linux PR_SET_PDEATHSIG with SIGKILL, checks that the original
parent is still present, and replaces itself with the verified media executable.
An API worker crash therefore terminates its directly owned media child. Normal
request cancellation and probe timeouts continue to kill and reap the child.
HLS stop also waits after escalating from terminate to kill before removing files.

The helper opens the selected executable and executes the same inode. It refuses
nonregular, set-ID and file-capability executables, and fails closed if capability
metadata cannot be checked. No shell or subprocess preexec callback is involved.
Only fixed launch-refusal text is written; command arguments are never included.
The helper file runs directly so it does not initialize application settings.

This is Linux-specific. The kernel binding follows the creating parent thread,
not all possible descendants; later credential changes can clear that binding.
The shipped media binaries must remain ordinary unprivileged executables. Tests
use actual isolated local child processes; packaged qualification additionally
uses synthetic local media and the shipped FFmpeg/FFprobe, not vendor cameras.

This component does not share HLS session metadata between API workers, route
media across containers or hosts, add per-viewer leases, remove files left after
an ungraceful worker exit, bound the FFmpeg diagnostic file, or solve recorded
session-key and cache invalidation. These remain separate release work.
